              host-registrars)))\n \n \n ;; =============================================================================\n@@ -590,9 +603,10 @@\n             exception\"\n     (let [registry (-> (module/empty-registry)\n                        module/register-cell-module\n-                       data/register-data-module\n+                       (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\n                        (integer/register-integer-module\n-                         {::integer/max-bits 60, ::integer/max-digits 5}))\n+                         {::integer/max-bits 60, ::integer/max-digits 5})\n+                       prelude/admit)\n           source (str \"d = {1: 'a'}\\n\"\n                       \"s = {1, 2}\\n\"\n                       \"try:\\n\"\ndiff --git a/test/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc b/test/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\nindex 9b94f94c..a82e45a1 100644\n--- a/test/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\n+++ b/test/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\n@@ -36,8 +36,9 @@\n    :primitives vm/primitives,\n    :modules (-> (module/empty-registry)\n                 module/register-cell-module\n-                data/register-data-module\n-                (prelude/register-integer-module limits))})\n+                (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\n+                (prelude/register-integer-module limits)\n+                prelude/admit)})\n \n \n (def ^:private load-semantic-ast\n[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"query_source\":\"sdk\"}\nWe're changing auto mode to no longer charge for classifier requests in Claude Code. However, this session isn't eligible because your requests go through open.bigmodel.cn, which isn't compatible with this update. Nothing breaks: auto mode keeps working, and its classifier requests are billed as before. To fix it and access the new version of auto mode, ask your gateway to implement: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/auto-mode-classifier-billing\n[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"query_source\":\"auto_mode\"}\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-07 10:05:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 17:05:00 +07\n\n# PROVISIONAL early gate review — Python C3 slice S7 (glm)\n\n**Verdict: APPROVE-WITH-NITS (PROVISIONAL).** The author is still working (no report; `tmp-s7/` and `test/zz_s7_scratch_test.clj` are scratch, ignored, and must not land). Reviewed the source design and test design only; no suites or python run. The `py/repeat` redesign, `data` two-arity, `admit`, and the `round` order fix are faithful to the binding design and CPython-correct everywhere I could check statically; the test fan-out and corpus design are strong. One finding is **blocking at landing** (a fixture-vs-ruling contradiction on the pow/round keyword item), plus owed in-flight items. Nothing found that requires re-architecting.\n\n## What verified clean (attack list, condensed)\n\n- **`py/repeat` order is exactly as ruled** (prelude.cljc:1015-1048): non-int → `TypeError`; the floor-div-mod quotient check implements the 64-bit Py_ssize_t window exactly (boundaries pinned: `-2^63` passes, `2^63` and `-2^63-1` refuse with `\"cannot fit 'int' into an index-sized integer\"`); size-0/negative → count 0 (so `'' * 2**62` → `''` without error); then exact `(integer/mul size count)` compared to `(data/max-items)` via `integer/compare`; greater → `(py/int-result :yin.vm.integer/bit-limit)`, which I verified builds **MemoryError with `(py/tuple [])` args** (prelude.cljc:1516-1522); only then the loop. The old `k > 2^53-1` pre-check is gone; the three remaining `9007199254740991` occurrences are pre-existing and unrelated (data.cljc:70-73 `max-safe`, lower.cljc:255-257 literal folding, prelude.cljc:1901-1902/1980-1981 range canonicalization). `*=` routes through `py/repeat` (py/imul, prelude.cljc:1059-1065; breach leaves the list untouched since `cell/set!` is outside the raising call). Loop counters are provably native (`count ≤ max-items < 2^53`), so no host primitive ever sees a carrier. `integer/mul` breaching the bit limit surfaces through the same `py/int-result` boundary — same class, as design 2.3 allows (\"the bit limit breaches first; same class\"), pinned under `small`.\n- **`data` two-arity** (data.cljc:541-576): export `max-items [0]` built like `integer/max-digits` (arity-checked `[0]`, `:pure` profile `[0] #{} :none`); one-arity exports no `max-items` (pinned in data_test.cljc:384-388); `check-limits!` enforces positive-native with no default (0, -1, 1.5, nil, \"1\", `{}` all refused, data_test.cljc:405-411).\n- **`admit`** (prelude.cljc:3026-3046): refusal datum exactly per design 2.2 (`:yang.python.antlr/refusal :yang.python.antlr/host-names`, `:missing` sorted list); I verified `resolve-module`'s walk returns nil for absent modules/exports (module.cljc:113-118, 40-55) so `some?` is the right test, and the `integer/name → integer.name` symbol rebuild matches `symbol->path`. `host-names` gains `data/max-items` (prelude.cljc:154).\n- **`round` order fix** (prelude.cljc:2734-2745) — see finding 3.\n- **No new `py.rt/*` state** (diff adds zero), no builtin class cells, engine/UCF/integer module untouched, S6 files untouched (only prelude.cljc + data.cljc under src).\n- **Fan-out**: every Python composition I could find registers `{::data/max-items 1048576}` and calls `admit`, including the shared `e2e/host-registry` (e2e_test.clj:60-84) that covers e2e_c1/e2e_c2/int_ops_parser, and `opts-under` for both wide and `small` runners (int_ops_test.cljc:25-35). Non-Python compositions (data_test one-arity uses) correctly exempt.\n- **Corpus generator** asserts 3.9.6, ASCII sources, writes from repo root, hand pins labelled by `origin`/`profile` columns; programs ≤ ~40 rows each (≪ 400); `^:slow` + `slow/guard` on the gate, parser, and boundary tests; drift test binds packets↔parser↔docstrings (c3_gate_parser_test.clj:33-43). Traps respected: `#?(:cljd ... :clj ... :cljs ...)` cljd-first everywhere, NaN as `inf - inf`, no `0.0`/`1.0` row literals, big ints via `integer/` ops. Hand-pin arithmetic spot-verified (`int('9'*4300) % 7 = 3`, `2^128-1`, `3·2^62−2^63`, `2^60−1` hex).\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **MAJOR — blocking at landing: the pow/round keyword ledger item is self-contradictory in-tree.** The binding design 2.4 (from S4 gate finding 2, collab/1791230000000…: \"CPython 3.9.6's `pow` and `round` are positional-only\") rules the fix as `:no-kw true` on both specs; the specs still lack it (prelude.cljc:2868 `(py/make-function \"pow\" {:params [\"base\" \"exp\" \"mod\"]}`, :2877 `\"round\"`). Meanwhile the new fixtures assert the **opposite**: `int-conv-v1.generate.py:47-48,53-54` measure `pow(base=a, exp=b)` / `round(number=a, ndigits=b)` as **successes**, committed as `int-conv-v1.txt:1013-1016` (`pow_kw 2 10 1024`, `round_kw 1250 -2 1200`, …), and the c3 `conversions` program runs `print(pow(base=2, exp=10), round(number=1250, ndigits=-2))` outside any try/except with `c3-corpus-v1.txt:194/213` recording `| 1024 1200`. Sharp diagnostic: if 3.9.6 refuses keyword calls, the int-conv rows would have been `!TypeError …` (the generator catches per-op) and **the c3 generator would crash outright** (uncaught TypeError inside `exec`), so the committed .txt implies either an accepting 3.9.6 or a fixture not produced by the generator. **Exact fix:** settle it first — `/usr/bin/python3 -I -c \"print(pow(base=2, exp=10))\"` (read-only probe). If TypeError (expected per the binding design and my own reading of 3.9's clinic `/`): add `:no-kw true` at prelude.cljc:2868 and :2877, move the conversions kw line under `try/except TypeError` (or drop it), regenerate both fixtures with the 3.9.6 interpreter (rows become `!TypeError pow() takes no keyword arguments`-class), and keep `keyword-op`'s expectations aligned. If it genuinely answers 1024: the binding ruling is wrong — get an architect re-rule recorded in 8.5.4 before landing. Either way the report must state fixture provenance (which interpreter produced each .txt); per the S4 precedent the orchestrator's regeneration diff is the gate.\n2. **MINOR — lint-scope ledger item landed as a comment, not a docstring.** `int_conv_test.cljc:358-362` puts the ruled scope statement (\"`prelude/function-definitions`, the core helpers only…\") in `;;` comments above `string-literal-lint-test`; design 2.4 and S4 finding 3 ask for the *docstring*. Exact fix: `(deftest string-literal-lint-test \"Scope: prelude/function-definitions …\" ...)`.\n3. **NOTE — approved deviation from the design's letter: `round`'s non-int-ndigits check sits after dispatch on `x`, not \"before dispatching on `x`\" (design 2.4).** The engineer's placement (index-TypeError inside the int and float branches; `__round__`-absent TypeError in the else, prelude.cljc:2734-2745) matches CPython's actual order — builtin `round` looks up `__round__` before converting ndigits — and the new measured rows pin it (`round_int s:61 … → \"type str doesn't define __round__ method\"`, int-conv-v1.txt:1018). Taken literally the design's wording would diverge from CPython for `round(obj, 1.5)`. No code change; record the correction in the S7 report and doc so the ruling table is fixed.\n4. **OWED (in-flight, keep on the landing checklist) — golden re-mints.** `float_address_test.cljc:223-229` pins the prelude root blake3 `460cf5b8…` and :246-267 pin A/A'/record/prelude-subtree (`3c8681d9…`) — all stale after the `py/repeat` and `round` row edits; only the hook-prelude golden (`2d6019…`, :228) should survive unchanged (hooks/uast untouched — verify). Deferring until the prelude is final is correct sequencing (especially with the `:no-kw` edits still owed), but design 2.5's re-mint and the safepoint/lower/lower_portable \"expected unchanged, checked\" pass must not be dropped. The 8.5.4 doc edits (ruling 11 and 14 paragraphs, conversions-list rows, PM2 sentence, accepted divergences, prelude docstring) and the seven ruling-14 mutation records are likewise not yet present — expected mid-flight; the landing gate checks them.\n5. **OWED (sequencing, by design) — PM2's Node/Dart legs cannot pass in this worktree.** `an-image-carries-no-profile-test` (int_contract_test.cljc) resumes a version-1 body holding 2^100 through `handoff/resume-task`; the decoder here has no `jing.cbor/numeric?` arm (handoff.cljc:1430-1436), so JS/Dart refuse `:host-object` until S6 lands and S7 rebases (design §3). Fine — but re-run and say so in the report.\n6. **NIT — `read-corpus` silently drops blank lines everywhere** (c3_gate_test.cljc:37 `remove str/blank?`). The generator asserts non-blank *source* lines (`assert all(lines)`) but not non-blank *stdout* lines; a future bare `print()` would vanish from the fixture and fail confusingly. Exact fix: `assert all(stdout[:-1].split('\\n'))` in c3-corpus-v1.generate.py (and the int-conv generator's row lines are already non-blank by construction).\n7. **NIT (optional) — the `data` two-arity ignores unknown limit keys** (data.cljc:566-576): `{::data/max-items 5, ::data/typo 1}` registers silently. The integer registrar behaves the same way, so this is consistent; a `(doseq [k (keys limits)] …)` whitelist check is a one-liner if strictness is wanted.\n\n**Bottom line:** source design approved as designed; the one thing I would not let move without resolution is finding 1 — regenerate the fixtures under a verified 3.9.6, reconcile with the `:no-kw` ruling (or get the re-rule), and record provenance. Re-review at the landing gate should check findings 1, 2, 4, and 5 first.\ntarget/profile/changed-cljd.log:172:SKIP slow test pinning-test (set DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 to run)\ntarget/profile/changed-cljd.log:175:SKIP slow test cell-refusal-test (set DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 to run)\ntarget/profile/changed-cljd.log:178:SKIP slow test collection-test (set DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 to run)\ntarget/profile/changed-cljd.log:183:SKIP slow test float-text-fixture-test (set DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 t